    {
      "id": 11,
      "title": "Federal Funds Rate",
      "description": "The interest rate at which banks lend money to each other overnight, set by the Federal Reserve. This is the Fed's primary lever for controlling the economy — raising it slows spending and fights inflation, lowering it stimulates growth during downturns. Every other interest rate you encounter (credit cards, car loans, mortgages, savings accounts) moves roughly in the same direction as this one. The sharp drops on this chart mark the Fed's emergency responses to crises: the dot-com bust in 2001, the financial crisis in 2008, and the pandemic in 2020. The aggressive rise from 2022 to 2023 was the fastest rate-hiking cycle in 40 years, aimed squarely at bringing down the post-pandemic inflation surge.",
