      "description": "A broad measure of how much money exists in the U.S. economy, in billions of dollars. M2 includes physical cash, checking and savings accounts, and money market funds. The Federal Reserve influences M2 primarily through bond purchases (quantitative easing, which injects money) and bond sales (quantitative tightening, which removes it). When M2 grows rapidly, more dollars are chasing the same amount of goods and services, which tends to push prices higher. The extraordinary surge in 2020-2021 — the fastest M2 growth since World War II, driven by pandemic stimulus and Fed bond buying — is widely cited as a primary cause of the inflation wave that followed. The subsequent flattening and slight decline in 2022-2023 reflects the Fed reversing course.",
